          "The Volkswagen Beetle originated from a direct commission Adolf Hitler gave engineer Ferdinand Porsche in 1934: design a 'people's car' ('Volkswagen,' literally) that was simple, reliable, and cheap enough for any German family to afford, a project that produced only a handful of civilian units before the factory was fully converted to military production during World War II. After the war ended, with the factory under British military control and nearly in ruins, an army officer decided to restart production of the small car as part of Germany's economic reconstruction, and what had begun as a Nazi propaganda project ended up, paradoxically, becoming one of the most recognizable symbols of the American hippie and counterculture movement of the 1960s and '70s, thanks to its accessible, affordable, deliberately unglamorous design set against the large, showy cars typical of the era. Produced almost continuously between 1938 and 2003, with more than 21 million units built across factories on several continents, the original Beetle became the best-selling single car design in history, even surpassing the Ford Model T, before Volkswagen finally discontinued its original Mexican production line in 2003.",
